# Overview
#
# A vague criterion is a phrase that leaves the reader to supply a
# criterion the writer knows (`set the timeout as needed`). The fix
# names the criterion (`set the timeout longer than the slowest
# request`), or drops the phrase when every case applies. The rule sits
# at Recommended, because a listed phrase can also name the criterion
# itself.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists each phrase. `as required` counts only without `by` after
# it. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the guard sits
# glued to `required` and reads the token right after it. The phrase at
# the end of a line therefore still counts.
#
# False Positives
#
# `as required by the spec` names the criterion, so `as required` passes
# before `by`.
#
# A description of lazy evaluation still draws a finding (`computes
# values when needed`), because there the phrase names the criterion.
#
# The rule still reports `if possible` before a noun that `possible`
# describes (`drop the index if possible duplicates remain`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseVagueCriterion holds the phrases (`as needed`,
# `where applicable`), and the Optional ProseVagueCriterion holds the
# single words (`appropriate`, `properly`).
#
# ProseTrailingCondition skips `if needed`, `if necessary`,
# `if applicable`, `if appropriate`, and `if possible`, so only this
# rule reports them. This rule matches such a phrase only when little
# sits between its two words. Only emphasis, a footnote reference, a
# span in single tildes or in carets, or the markup of a markdown link
# can sit there (`if **needed**`, `if[^1] needed`, `if ~x~ needed`,
# `if ^x^ needed`, `if [needed](https://example.com)`). Harper shows only
# the text after the `|` of a wikilink, so the target before the `|`
# counts as such markup too (`if [[page|needed]]`).
#
# ProseTrailingCondition skips the phrase in that case, and it reports
# the `if` in `if "needed"`, `if (needed)`, `if [needed]`, and
# `if, needed`. Harper drops a span in single tildes or in carets along
# with the words in it, so ProseTrailingCondition also reports the `if`
# in `if ~needed~` and `if ^needed^`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 173 lines in the measure.py corpus. In 17 of 20 sampled
# hits, the phrase left the criterion unstated, or the sentence read the
# same without the phrase. In two of the misfires, `as required` named
# its criterion after `for` or `per` (`as required for JSON object
# names`). In the third misfire, `if necessary` pointed at a state that
# the same sentence named (`ensures that a value is always addressable
# (by copying the value if necessary)`).
#
# `if possible` hit 16 of those lines. In 15 of the 16, the phrase left
# the criterion unstated, or the sentence read the same without the
# phrase. In the one misfire, `if possible` pointed at a check that the
# same sentence named (`See if the statement has already been prepared
# on this connection, and reuse it if possible`).
expr main [(as needed), (as necessary), (as appropriate), (as applicable), (as required!(( )by)), (where needed), (where necessary), (where appropriate), (where applicable), (when needed), (when necessary), (when appropriate), (when applicable), (if needed), (if necessary), (if appropriate), (if applicable), (if possible)]
let message "Vague criterion. Name the condition the reader should check, or drop the phrase."
let description "Flags `as needed`, `where applicable`, `if possible`, and similar phrases that leave the reader to supply a criterion."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Set the timeout longer than the slowest request."
allows "Pin the version as required by the release policy."
